Yes. For a .NET WPF project, there are two separate challenges:

Building the project on Xubuntu.
Running the resulting WPF application on Linux.

The best approach depends heavily on which .NET version you're targeting.

First: Check Your Project Type

Open your .csproj file and look for something like:

<TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>

or

<TargetFramework>net6.0-windows</TargetFramework>

or

<TargetFrameworkVersion>v4.8</TargetFrameworkVersion>
Scenario A: Modern .NET (6/7/8/9)

Example:

<TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
<UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
Scenario B: .NET Framework

Example:

<TargetFrameworkVersion>v4.8</TargetFrameworkVersion>

The setup differs significantly.

Recommended Option for Modern WPF (.NET 6+)

For modern SDK-style WPF projects, use:

Native Linux .NET SDK
Wine only for running/testing

This is usually the cleanest solution.

Step 1: Install .NET SDK

On Xubuntu:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install wget apt-transport-https

Add Microsoft repository:

wget https://packages.microsoft.com/config/ubuntu/24.04/packages-microsoft-prod.deb
sudo dpkg -i packages-microsoft-prod.deb
sudo apt update

Install SDK:

sudo apt install dotnet-sdk-8.0

Verify:

dotnet --info
Step 2: Enable Windows Targeting

Modern WPF projects target Windows.

Build with:

dotnet build -p:EnableWindowsTargeting=true

or:

dotnet publish \
  -r win-x64 \
  -c Release \
  -p:EnableWindowsTargeting=true

The Linux SDK can often compile Windows-targeted projects because the reference assemblies are downloaded by NuGet.

Step 3: Install Wine

Enable 32-bit support:

sudo dpkg --add-architecture i386
sudo apt update

Install Wine:

sudo apt install wine64 wine32

Check:

wine --version
Step 4: Create Wine Prefix
export WINEPREFIX=$HOME/.wine-wpf
wineboot
Step 5: Install .NET Runtime in Wine

For self-contained deployments, you can skip this.

Publish:

dotnet publish \
  -c Release \
  -r win-x64 \
  --self-contained true

This bundles the runtime.

Step 6: Run Your WPF App
wine MyApp.exe

or

cd bin/Release/net8.0-windows/win-x64/publish
wine MyApp.exe
Recommended Option for .NET Framework 4.x WPF

If you're targeting .NET Framework 4.8:

<TargetFrameworkVersion>v4.8</TargetFrameworkVersion>

then Linux compilation is much harder.

You generally need:

Wine
Visual Studio Build Tools running under Wine

or

A Windows VM

The Windows VM is usually more reliable.

Alternative: Use Mono

Install:

sudo apt install mono-complete

Build:

msbuild MyProject.sln

However:

WPF support in Mono is incomplete
Many WPF applications won't build correctly
Modern WPF features often fail

I generally do not recommend Mono for serious WPF work in 2025+.

Better Alternative: Use a Windows Build Container or VM

If your goal is CI/CD or reproducible builds, these are usually better than Wine.

Option 1: Windows VM

Install:

VirtualBox
KVM/QEMU

Create:

Windows 11 VM
Visual Studio Build Tools

Build inside VM.

Pros:

Nearly 100% compatibility.

Cons:

Uses more disk and RAM.
Option 2: GitHub Actions

Build Windows artifacts in CI:

runs-on: windows-latest

Then from Linux:

git push

and download the produced artifacts.

This is often the easiest solution.

Can WPF Run Reliably on Linux?

This is the bigger limitation.

WPF depends on:

DirectX
Windows Presentation Foundation APIs
Windows desktop infrastructure

Wine compatibility varies by application.

Simple WPF apps:

Often work.

Complex apps:

Hardware acceleration issues.
Rendering glitches.
DPI problems.
Third-party control failures.

If Linux support is important long-term, consider migrating UI layers to:

Avalonia UI
Uno Platform

These run natively on Linux and Windows.

My Practical Recommendation

If your project is WPF on .NET 6/7/8/9:

Install the Linux .NET SDK.
Build with:
dotnet build -p:EnableWindowsTargeting=true
Publish self-contained:
dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained true
Install Wine.
Run the generated .exe with Wine.

If you tell me:

your target framework (net8.0-windows, net48, etc.),
Xubuntu version,
whether you're using Visual Studio solution files or only a single project,

I can give exact commands tailored to your project.
